Subject: register VM: decide a strict compare with an undefined operand from the tag instead of the strict-equality helper

What this does:
  box2d's fused compare-and-branches are dominated by the default-argument
  guard `x === undefined` / `x !== undefined`: of ~9.5 M strict compares on the
  fixed.js kernel, ~8.9 M have an undefined operand. The generic handler reaches
  them the long way -- after the both-int, both-float and mixed-numeric blocks
  have been tried and rejected, through an out-of-line call to regvm_strict_eq,
  which is emitted with a six-register spill around a ~40-instruction path.

  The undefined operand is not a compile-time constant the encoder can drop: it
  is a global read (`ROP_get_global`) whose value is undefined only at run time,
  so a dedicated opcode cannot be selected at lowering. (A first attempt that
  did exactly that emitted nothing; the `undefined` literal form is already
  folded to `OP_is_undefined` by the stack bytecode pass at compile time.)
  Instead the fast path is taken on the runtime tag, ahead of the numeric
  blocks:

      if ((_sub & ~REG_CMP_INVERT) == REG_BIN_STRICT_EQ || ...STRICT_NEQ) {
          JSValue ... _ta = JS_VALUE_GET_TAG(_a), _tb = JS_VALUE_GET_TAG(_b);
          if (likely(_ta == JS_TAG_UNDEFINED || _tb == JS_TAG_UNDEFINED)) {
              int _se = (_ta == JS_TAG_UNDEFINED && _tb == JS_TAG_UNDEFINED)
                            ^ ((_sub & ~REG_CMP_INVERT) == REG_BIN_STRICT_NEQ);
              int _take = _se ^ ((_sub & REG_CMP_INVERT) != 0);
              ...
          }
      }

  A strict comparison against undefined is true exactly when both operands are
  undefined, so one tag test replaces the helper call. The `!==` and the
  branch's inverted sense fold into the same `_se ^ inv` the generic strict
  block uses. The non-undefined strict compares and every other sub-op fall
  through unchanged.

  The macro is instantiated for `branch_cmp_reg_reg` and every per-sense narrow
  opcode; for the per-sense forms `_sub` is a compile-time constant and the new
  test folds away, so only `branch_cmp_reg_reg(_s)` pays for the extra compare.
  The monolith's separate REGVM_CMP_BRANCH macro is left as it is (it is the
  THREAD=0 path, not the measured one).

  Measured (`fixed.js`, 32 iterations, i7-8700): 24,139,025,404 ->
  23,820,723,349 instructions (**-0.318 G, -1.32 %**) and 11,251,225,965 ->
  11,164,235,943 cycles (~-0.8 %), against the plan's 0.4 G expected / 0.2 G
  floor for this item. Gate run: 23,821,625,829 / 11,206,315,155. Checksum
  `546.1008391043414` exact; diff-suite `pass=398 fail=0 skip=0` mono and
  threaded; test262 50/81192 errors (<= 50, baseline equal); ASan+UBSan box2d
  clean and the strict-equals/equals/if test262 directories 0/144 errors with no
  sanitizer reports; leak assertion clean.

No opcode, operand encoding or serialized register-format change.
